T|               FADE IN:
O|
S|               INT. VICTORIAN ESTATE - 1895 - DAY
O|
N,G|               CLOSE UP: Ornate antique GRANDFATHER CLOCK strikes 2:15.
O|
N|               The credits begin to roll.
O|
N|               Calligraphy place cards are painstakingly hand-written in 
N|               preparation for a formal reception.
O|
N|               Servants meticulously clean an elaborate crystal chandelier.
O|
N|               Hands rapidly shine silver cutlery.
O|
N|               Fresh flowers arranged in several enormous vases.
O|
S|               INT. KITCHEN - DAY
O|
N|               Kitchen clock strikes 2:15.
O|
N|               Poultry plucked. Feathers everywhere.
O|
N|               Steaming copper pots boiling over. Platters prepared with 
N|               decorative garnish.
O|
N|               Extravagant tiered cake is lavishly iced. A glob of frosting 
N|               enters a pair of puckered lips. The perpetrator, a fey, 
N|               pompous, HEAD VALET, seriously considers the texture and 
N|               flavor of the icing, then gives the PASTRY CHEF a withering, 
N|               sour look.
O|
N|               The valet, oblivious to the chef's fury, gathers a prepared 
N|               silver breakfast tray and checks his pocket watch. 2:15.
O|
S|               INT. BEDROOM - DAY
O|
N|               Antique bedside table clock -- just past 2:15. The valet 
N|               throws open velvet drapes, sunlight FLOODS the room revealing 
N|               a figure sleeping soundly in a massive oak framed bed. The 
N|               valet puts down the tray, pulls back the quilt.
O|
N|               The dozing figure gives the valet a deliberate, angry KICK.
O|
N|               Flinching infinitesimally, the valet crosses to the closet, 
N|               selects three suits and holds them up for inspection. From 
N|               beneath the puffiest of pillows, AN EYE reluctantly opens. A 
N|               weary arm points decisively to the riding outfit.
O|
S|               EXT. ESTATE - DAY
O|
N,I|               The riding outfit is adorned by LEOPOLD, Duke of Albany, a 
N,I|               dashing, virile man in his 30's. GALLOPING with great abandon, 
N|               he expertly leads his horse up hills and over hedges. He 
N|               sends gardeners shaping the grounds fleeing from his path.
O|
N|               In the background, lanterns are being strung throughout the 
N|               estate. Rugs are beaten out of open windows.
O|
S|               INT. HALLWAY - EVENING
O|
N|               Large hallway clock -- 6:15. A pair of boots track a freshly 
N|               scrubbed floor as Leopold heads carelessly up the stairway. 
N|               Three steps behind, the valet snaps his fingers signaling 
N|               the staff to clean up the mess.
O|
N,G|               SERIES OF CLOSE UPS
O|
N|               Gaudily framed portraits of somber relations.
O|
N|               A bird sits listless in a gilded cage.
O|
N|               Hot water is poured into a bath.
O|
N|               Carriages begin to arrive.
O|
N|               Orchestra leader signals the musicians to play.
O|
N|               Guests fill the ballroom in formal attire.
O|
S|               INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT
O|
N|               Leopold stares haughtily into a full length mirror.
O|
N|               He wears only dotted linen drawers and a black silk top hat 
N|               of the period. He flexes slightly. The valet hastily fastens 
N|               a corset around his mid-section from behind.
O|
N|               Leopold eyes his reflection seductively. He becomes bored.
O|
N|               He turns to his right. The valet spoons sorbet from a silver 
N|               saucer into his mouth. He turns back to the mirror to watch 
N|               himself eat. The valet pats his mouth dry.
O|
N|               He affects a pampered STRUT, crossing the room as the valet 
N|               sprays a mixture of perfume and powder into the air. Leopold 
N|               thrusts out his arms walking through the mist, turns and 
N|               saunters back to his favorite place, in front of the mirror.
O|
N|               Finally, as if weighing a great decision, Leopold hands his 
N|               hat to his valet and nods firmly into the mirror.
O|
N,G|               CLOSE UP
O|
N|               Fine linen undervest slides over his head.
O|
N|               Black twilled worsted trousers pulled up to his waist.
O|
N|               The buttoning of his hard, white, roll collar.
O|
N|               A cream suede glove stitched in black slides over his hand.
O|
N|               A perfect tight bow tie is tied around his neck.
O|
N|               An elegant black tuxedo jacket with silk facings and tails 
N|               to the knees is fitted to his torso.
O|
N|               His hair is sleeked with oil and centrally parted.
O|
N|               His eyebrows are brushed with a tiny silver comb.
O|
N|               His cheeks are pinched.
O|
N|               A red silk handkerchief is placed in the bosom of his lapel.
O|
N|               Leopold stares into the mirror. The effect is DAZZLING. He 
N|               raises one eyebrow. Then, in a voice you could pour over a 
N|               waffle:
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         You dance like a herd of cattle, 
U|                         Miss Fairchild. You are a rare woman 
U|                         who lights up the room... simply by 
U|                         leaving it.
O|
N,I|               He bows. As he rises, the reflection of Leopold's UNCLE OTTO, 
N,I|               a pampered, effectual man in his 60's, appears behind Leopold 
N|               in the looking glass.
O|
C|                                     UNCLE OTTO
U|                         Tell me Leopold, do you plan to join 
U|                         us downstairs after completing this 
U|                         one-sided flash of wit?
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         I despise affairs of this sort, Uncle 
U|                         Otto. No one really listens to anyone 
U|                         else. If you attempt it, you will 
U|                         see why.
O|
C|                                     UNCLE OTTO
U|                         You judge everything too severely. I 
U|                         wonder what would happen if you were 
U|                         to cast that critical eye inward?
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         You do not wonder at all. You merely 
U|                         ask in order to state your own 
U|                         opinion.
O|
C|                                     UNCLE OTTO
U|                         We are all concerned about you.
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         Ahh, here it comes.
O|
C|                                     UNCLE OTTO
U|                         Your life does not seem to have any 
U|                         direction. At the age of 25 a man is 
U|                         too young to do anything well. At 35 
U|                         he is too old. Now is your time to 
U|                         take action. Tell me, what is it you 
U|                         want to DO with your life?
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
P|                              (wearily)
U|                         I do not know.
O|
C|                                     UNCLE OTTO
P|                              (just as weary)
U|                         Your ignorance cramps my conversation.
P|                              (he slaps Leopold's 
P|                              face twice rather 
P|                              quickly)
U|                         Wake up! This is your one and only 
U|                         life. Every man has the chance to 
U|                         make a difference. DO something!
O|
N|               Leopold, stung, takes a moment to compose himself.
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         I am most grateful for your 
U|                         surveillance and for your sympathy. 
U|                         I assure you there is no need for 
U|                         concern. I am as effectual and 
U|                         attentive... as my position allows.
O|
C|                                     UNCLE OTTO
U|                         Life is not position, Leopold. It is 
U|                         action.
P|                              (he straightens 
P|                              Leopold's tie)
U|                         I will see you downstairs promptly. 
U|                         In the appropriate frame of mind.
O|
N|               He leaves. Leopold stares into the mirror, snaps his fingers. 
N|               The valet hands him a glass and pours a small shot of brandy. 
N|               Leopold shoots him a look. The valet fills the glass higher.
O|
N|               Leopold drains the glass and heads to the doorway. He 
N|               hesitates, taking a deep breath and a long pause. Finally...
O|
C|                                     THE VALET
P|                              (grandly)
U|                         Go spread joy.
O|
N|               Without looking back, Leopold leaves the room.
O|
S|               INT. BALLROOM - NIGHT
O|
N|               Music fills the air as the creme de la creme of Victorian 
N|               society gather inside. Leopold holds a prearranged dance 
N|               card. He looks at the names and finds his first partner is 
N|               MISS TREE. Leopold looks upward helplessly at an unrelenting 
N|               God.
O|
S|               INT. BALLROOM DANCE FLOOR - NIGHT
O|
N|               A profoundly self-conscious Leopold dances energetically 
N,G,I|               into frame with MISS TREE, an ancient, elfin, yet amazingly 
N,I|               SPRY woman who gapes up at him searchingly, unblinking.
O|
N|               Leopold opens his mouth to speak, can think of nothing 
N|               suitable to say, and clamps his mouth shut. They dance out 
N,G|               of frame.
O|
S|               INT. BALLROOM DANCE FLOOR - NIGHT
O|
N,G,I|               Leopold dances into frame with CLARA, a BEAUTIFUL girl of 
N,I|               19. He glances at her and smiles. She titters, turning away 
N|               shyly. They whirl around the dance floor. His smile grows 
N|               into a broad grin. Caught up in the excitement, Clara beams 
N|               up at him... happily revealing a mouth full of missing, inky 
N|               teeth.
O|
N|               Leopold, face frozen in a formal smile, dances on.
O|
S|               INT. BALLROOM DANCE FLOOR - NIGHT
O|
N,G,I|               Leopold lumbers into frame with MISS FAIRCHILD, a woman of 
N,I|               immense girth. With great dignity and extreme difficulty he 
N|               maneuvers her around the floor, looking everywhere except 
N|               her enormous bosom.
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         You dance like an angel, Miss 
U|                         Fairchild. You light up the room. I 
U|                         thank you.
O|
N|               Miss Fairchild curtseys heavily. Leopold glances at his card, 
N|               sees the name Miss Serena Clacket and shudders.
O|
N|               He spots a VERY LOVELY WOMAN hidden amongst the guests. She 
N|               scribbles in a small note pad, occasionally glancing around. 
N|               Hiding his dance card, he saunters over.
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         Good evening. I do not believe I 
U|                         have had the pleasure.
O|
N|               The young woman stares vacantly at Leopold until she realizes 
N|               he is addressing her. She puts her pad away.
O|
C|                                     CARLSON
U|                         ...Julia Carlson.
O|
N|               Leopold takes her hand and kisses it gently.
O|
C|                                     CARLSON
U|                         I'm sorry. Will you excuse me?
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
P|                              (slightly tipsy)
U|                         You are not from the area, are you?
O|
C|                                     CARLSON
P|                              (smiles and shakes 
P|                              her head "No")
U|                         Are you?
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         This is my estate.
O|
C|                                     CARLSON
U|                         Really?
P|                              (looking around the 
P|                              room)
U|                         Well it's not home but it's much...
O|
N|               Leopold regards her curiously. The orchestra starts up. There 
N|               is a hubbub amongst the guests.
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         Shocking! The TA-RA-RA-BOOM-DE-AY.
O|
C|                                     CARLSON
U|                         The? Oh, that's from Paris, isn't 
U|                         it?
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         It is a scandal nonetheless.
O|
N|               Leopold spots Uncle Otto observing him from across the room.
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
U|                         Miss Carlson, do me the honor?
O|
C|                                     CARLSON
U|                         I'm afraid I'm not much of a dancer.
O|
C|                                     LEOPOLD
P|                              (smiling seductively)
U|                         Truly I would be in your debt. Any 
U|                         admonition would be erased simply by 
